>>Doors.. The *only* thing I would like to be changed is that you *can't* see
>>beforehand if the door is open or closed! This can be serious if you are
>>running away from a monster. Then you bump into one and loose a turn!
I tried this afterwards. You DON'T loose a turn.

>IMHO this is an EXCELLENT idea!  Any ideas how (i.e. what symbols to use)?
>/ and \ come to mind but these already mean something.  Then again + also
>means something different when not used in conjunction with a wall.

I don't think we should use '/' or '\' or '~' as a door! '/' is a wand. (Oh,
didn't you know? :-) and '~' is a long worm's tail over a door.. '\' isn't
supported on all terminals, I think. 

>or how about

>     ---#---  no door or an open door and ---+--- a closed door?

This has some idea in it! Though I still prefer the idea of highlighting
a closed/locked door.

New and improved idea!
How about:
'#' is no door.
'+' is open door.
'+'(highlighted) is closed/locked door.
These symbols reflect reality. You CAN see the difference between a
no/open/closed door, but not the difference between a closed/locked door!
These symbols are also familiar to all hackers. You don't usually think twice
over stepping on a '#'..

This would make rooms look like this:

     -----
   --|...+##############
   |^+...|
   --|...|
     |...###
     ----- ##

Oh, that on the left side is a closet containing an express elevator trap. 
Don't take any notice..:)
